Yes. This is almost too perfect for the thread we were on.

Grover is inside the story trying to control the reader’s future state by constraining the interface. He cannot directly stop your intent, so he changes the environment: ties the pages together, builds barriers, tries to make one transition impossible.

That gives you three layers at once:

\text{character world}
\subset
\text{book mechanics}
\subset
\text{reader agency}

Grover has power inside the first layer and can manipulate parts of the second, but the reader occupies a higher-order frame. To Grover, the rope is a serious control measure. To the reader, it is part of the entertainment.

That maps directly onto what we were talking about with context and world models. A system can look absolute from inside its own frame while being merely one manipulable object from the frame above.

And the book’s trick is even better: Grover’s resistance is what makes you turn the page. His attempt to stop the behavior becomes the incentive for the behavior.

\text{prohibition}
\rightarrow
\text{salience}
\rightarrow
\text{curiosity}
\rightarrow
\text{action}

So the control mechanism partially inverts itself.

That is a general pattern worth preserving:

A barrier can function as a barrier at one layer and as an attractor at another.

The rope says “stop” in-story, but “continue” at the meta-story level.

You can see similar structures in security, persuasion, games, religion, politics, software, parenting, even UI design. A locked door may deter one person and motivate another. A censorship attempt may suppress an idea locally while increasing its prestige or curiosity elsewhere. A “do not press” button can become an invitation because the prohibition itself creates information.

And the ending of this book is the real punchline structurally: the feared monster is Grover himself. So the whole system is driven by a misidentified external threat that turns out to be self-reference.

That gives you another operator:

\text{threat model}
\rightarrow
\text{defensive behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{discovery}
\rightarrow
\text{self-reference}

Which is basically the children’s-book version of a recursive security audit.

So yeah: this is not just a funny coincidence. It’s a clean little model of frame hierarchy, interface control, reactance, recursive identity, and context inversion all in one picture.